# Project Overview

This repository is a Vite + React + TypeScript userscript project built with `vite-plugin-monkey`.

The current build pipeline treats the React app as a userscript entrypoint rather than a normal SPA:

- `vite.config.ts` wires `vite-plugin-monkey` to `src/main.tsx`.
- The plugin emits a browser-installable userscript bundle into `dist/*.user.js`.
- The current userscript metadata matches `https://www.google.com/` and uses the Vite logo as its icon.

## Common commands

- Install dependencies: `pnpm install`
- Start Vite dev server: `pnpm dev`
- Build userscript bundle: `pnpm build`
- Preview Vite build output: `pnpm preview`
- Run TypeScript checking only: `pnpm exec tsc -b`

## Testing and linting

- There is currently no test runner configured in `package.json`.
- There is currently no lint script configured in `package.json`.
- There is no single-test command yet because no test framework is set up.

## Architecture

### Build/runtime model

This is not a conventional React app with a static `index.html` root. The userscript entry is `src/main.tsx`, and it creates a new `<div>` at runtime and appends it to `document.body` before mounting React. When changing mounting behavior, preserve that injected-root pattern unless the userscript integration is intentionally being redesigned.

### Source layout

- `src/main.tsx` is the userscript entrypoint and React bootstrap.
- `src/App.tsx` is the top-level React component.
- `src/App.css` and `src/index.css` hold component-level and global styling.
- `src/assets/` stores imported static assets bundled by Vite.

### TypeScript configuration

TypeScript is split into project references:

- `tsconfig.app.json` covers browser app code under `src/`.
- `tsconfig.node.json` covers Vite/tooling code, currently `vite.config.ts`.

Both configs use strict checking, `moduleResolution: "bundler"`, and `noEmit: true`. The production bundle is generated by Vite rather than by `tsc` emitting JS.

## Notes for future changes

- If you change userscript metadata such as match patterns, namespace, or icon, update `vite.config.ts`.
- If you need to change where the script runs or how it injects UI into the page, inspect both `vite.config.ts` and `src/main.tsx` together.
- The current `src/App.tsx` and styles are still the default starter UI, so most real feature work will likely replace that scaffold rather than extend it.
